    contentEn: `<h2>Getting from Sóller to Palma Airport</h2>
<p>A private transfer from Sóller to Palma de Mallorca Airport (PMI) covers around 30 kilometres and takes about 40 minutes by car, bringing you down out of the valley and its citrus groves, through the mountains, and across Palma to the terminal on the city's eastern edge. Your driver arrives at your accommodation in Sóller at the pickup time you specify, allowing a comfortable margin for the drive and check-in, so a trip that depends on mountain roads does not leave you rushing to make your flight.</p>
<p>The return route can run either through the Sóller Tunnel, the quicker option cut directly through the mountains, or over the older Coll de Sóller pass, a winding road with hairpin bends that takes longer but offers mountain views for anyone who prefers that route on the way out of the valley. A private driver will generally use the tunnel for a scheduled departure, since it offers the more predictable journey time, though the choice can be discussed when booking. You can check your flight's scheduled departure time on <a href="https://www.aena.es">aena.es</a> when planning your pickup, and arrange the whole journey — pickup point, flight number, and price — in advance through <a href="/get-a-quote/">get a quote</a>.</p>
<p>The same private service also covers your arrival, meeting you at the terminal when you first land and taking you up to Sóller through the mountains, so both directions of your stay can be arranged together in a single booking rather than sorted out separately as your trip approaches.</p>
<h3>About Palma de Mallorca Airport</h3>
<p>Palma de Mallorca Airport (PMI), also known as Son Sant Joan Airport, lies around 8 kilometres east of Palma city and is Spain's third-busiest airport, with a heavy schedule of departures running throughout the year and peaking during the summer months. Because your journey from Sóller starts with a descent through mountain terrain before reaching the flatter roads around Palma, building in extra time beyond the roughly 40-minute drive is sensible, particularly if your flight departs during a busy period of the day.</p>
<p>A driver experienced with both the tunnel and mountain-pass routes out of Sóller is well placed to judge realistic timings for your specific departure, adjusting for the time of day and any seasonal traffic around Palma itself.</p>
<h2>About Sóller</h2>
<p>Sóller occupies a wide valley on Mallorca's northwest coast, its slopes covered in terraced groves of orange and lemon trees that have shaped the town's identity for generations and still line the approach roads on the way out toward the mountains. The valley's sheltered position, enclosed by the peaks of the Serra de Tramuntana, gives it a noticeably different climate and character from the coastal towns elsewhere on the island.</p>
<p>Before heading to the airport, many visitors take a final ride on the Ferrocarril de Sóller, the historic vintage train that has linked the town to Palma since the early twentieth century, or a trip on the old wooden tram that connects the town centre to Port de Sóller, both departing from stops close together near the main square, the Plaça de la Constitució.</p>
<p>Sóller's identity is closely tied to these two historic rail links and to the citrus groves that surround the town, a combination that sets it apart from Mallorca's coastal resorts and gives visitors leaving the valley a genuinely different island experience to reflect on during the drive back to the airport.</p>
<p>Much of Sóller's townscape reflects a wealthier chapter of this citrus history: in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, many local merchants made fortunes trading oranges in France and returned to build grand townhouses in the Art Nouveau style then fashionable in Barcelona. The most striking result is the Banco de Sóller building on the main square, designed by Joan Rubió i Bellver, a disciple of Antoni Gaudí, its ornate façade a world away from the plain stone architecture found in most Mallorcan valley towns. The Ferrocarril de Sóller itself was largely funded by this same generation of returning merchants, and its route through thirteen tunnels cut into the mountains remains one of the more remarkable feats of early twentieth-century engineering on the island, still carrying the same character today as when it first opened in 1912.</p>
<h3>Leaving the Tramuntana Behind</h3>
<p>The drive out of Sóller toward the airport means leaving the Serra de Tramuntana, the UNESCO World Heritage-listed mountain range that encloses the valley, a landscape shaped by centuries of terraced agriculture and water management still visible on the slopes as the road climbs toward the tunnel or the mountain pass.</p>
<p>Whichever route your driver takes, the transition from the enclosed, green valley around Sóller to the open coastal plain near the airport is one of the more noticeable changes in scenery available on a single transfer anywhere on the island, and it is worth looking back toward the mountains before they disappear from view.</p>
<h3>Early-Morning and Late-Night Departures from Sóller</h3>
<p>Because Sóller sits on the far side of the Tramuntana from the airport, travellers with very early or very late flights sometimes worry about arranging transport at hours when public options are limited or non-existent. A private driver is available regardless of the hour, whether that means a pickup before dawn to make an early flight or a late-evening collection after a day spent exploring the valley has run later than planned.</p>
<p>For an early departure in particular, having a driver already booked and familiar with the tunnel route removes the need to plan an unfamiliar mountain drive yourself in the dark, or to hope a taxi is available in a town where options can be limited outside the main season. This is one of the more practical reasons travellers based in the valley choose to arrange both directions of their transfer well before their trip begins.</p>
<h2>What to Expect on the Journey to the Airport</h2>
<p>Because the route to the airport involves mountain roads before reaching the more predictable motorway network around Palma, a pre-arranged private driver who knows current road conditions is generally more reliable for a scheduled flight departure than estimating the drive yourself. Drivers plan pickup times with this in mind so you are not left cutting things close.</p>
<p>Guests travelling with luggage picked up during a stay in the valley, or with a child seat, should mention this when booking so the right vehicle is available for the return journey. It is worth arranging this ahead of time rather than on the day of departure.</p>
<h2>Planning Your Transfer</h2>
<p>Whether your stay in Sóller has centred on the town, the historic train, or trips out to Port de Sóller and the surrounding mountains, booking your return transfer in advance means one less thing to organise on your final day. See our <a href="/airport-transfers/">airport transfers</a> page for general departure details, and if your trip has included exploring more of Mallorca, our <a href="/hourly-chauffeur/">hourly chauffeur</a> service covers point-to-point travel whenever you need it.</p>`,
